def str2bool(value):
    """Parse an explicit command-line boolean while preserving '-f True/False'."""
    if isinstance(value, bool):
        return value
    normalized = str(value).strip().lower()
    if normalized in {"true", "t", "1", "yes", "y"}:
        return True
    if normalized in {"false", "f", "0", "no", "n"}:
        return False
    raise ArgumentTypeError("expected a boolean value: True or False")


CLI_EPILOG = r"""
Terminal adapter-search geometry
--------------------------------
The diagram below shows the LEFT alignment end. The RIGHT end is handled as
its mirror image. S = terminal soft-clipped query sequence; M = query sequence
already included in the mapped alignment.

Distances count read bases, not reference-genome positions. "Mapped" does
not imply perfect identity to the reference. Neither search-window setting
specifies a minimum number of adapter matches.

    terminal soft clip                 mapped alignment
    S S S S S S S S S S S S S S S S | M M M M M M M M M M M M M M M M
                                      ^
                                      mapped boundary

          <------ --check_in_soft ---->|<-- --check_in_match -->
          <----------- adapter-search window ------------------>

--check_in_soft determines how much terminal soft-clipped query sequence is
included in the local adapter-search window. --check_in_match extends the same
window into the immediately adjacent mapped query sequence. The latter is
important when adapter-derived bases have been aligned to the reference rather
than represented entirely as soft clipping.

After local alignment of the expected 5' or 3' terminal sequence, the hit must
also satisfy --check_from_alignment. This positional constraint requires the
adapter alignment to terminate sufficiently close to the mapped boundary on
the soft-clipped side; adapter alignments extending into the mapped side are
accepted only within the sequence included by --check_in_match. Thus a strong
adapter-like sequence match is not sufficient by itself: it must also occur at
the expected terminal position.

Worked example:
    With --check_in_soft 15 --check_in_match 10 --check_from_alignment 3,
    a 20-nt soft clip contributes its 15 bases nearest the mapped boundary;
    up to 10 adjacent aligned read bases complete the search window.
    A hit whose boundary-facing end stops 3 nt before the boundary passes
    the position test; one stopping 4 nt before it fails, whatever its score.
    A hit extending 2 nt into the aligned side passes position but still
    undergoes terminal artefact checks. A shorter soft clip contributes
    only the available bases. Hard-clipped sequence cannot be recovered.
    The conventional adapter-search path requires terminal soft clipping;
    --check_in_match alone does not enable searching an unclipped end.

Score and parameter interactions:
    Local alignment can recognise only part of the supplied adapter.
    Its score must be strictly GREATER than --five_score or --three_score;
    equality fails. Scores are not percentages or counts of matching bases.
    With +2 per match and a threshold of 16, eight perfectly paired bases
    score 16 and fail; nine score 18 and pass the score test. Position and
    terminal QC still apply.
    A larger search window exposes more sequence to possible false hits.
    More aligned-side sequence can recover partly aligned adapters, but
    also changes the sequence available to the short-homology check.
    A larger --check_from_alignment accepts more distant terminal hits.
    Choose adapter length and score thresholds together; these controls
    are not interchangeable.

False terminal-exon check
-------------------------
Adapter-derived sequence can occasionally be mapped as a short terminal exon.
--first_exon defines the maximum terminal-exon length subjected to this check,
and --match_in_first sets how much mapped terminal-exon sequence is compared
with the expected adapter. Sufficient adapter similarity causes the terminal
configuration to be treated as artefact-related rather than as a genuine exon.

Library-mode notes
------------------
For adapter-containing cDNA/CapTrap libraries, supply library-appropriate 5'
and 3' terminal sequences and score thresholds. The generic CLI defaults are
not intended to replace chemistry-specific settings. In --dRNA mode, the 5'
adapter search is skipped; mapper-derived strand information is used. Terminal
QC is retained for the true 3' end. --pacbio enables the optional adapterless
PacBio full-length cDNA orientation mode.

PacBio preprocessing and --pacbio:
    CCS generates consensus HiFi reads. In the standard Iso-Seq workflow,
    lima subsequently removes cDNA primers, including the 5' primer;
    isoseq refine can also remove the poly(A) tail. Do not attribute cDNA
    primer removal to CCS consensus generation itself.
    If the 5' primer has been removed but a detectable terminal poly(A)
    signal remains, use --pacbio for full-length PacBio cDNA reads.
    This mode searches both ends for the 3' signal. Exactly one end must
    receive a correct 3' call to infer orientation and accept the opposite
    end as 5', without requiring a 5' adapter hit. Full-length status is an
    input assumption, not independently established by this mode.
    If poly(A) has also been removed, --pacbio alone cannot replace the
    missing terminal evidence. Check preprocessing and retained sequence.
    Official preprocessing reference:
    https://isoseq.how/clustering/cli-workflow.html

Quick-start examples
--------------------
The examples below are library-specific starting configurations, not universal
platform presets. Adapter sequences must match the actual library preparation
and the sequence retained after preprocessing. Replace sample.sorted.bam,
output_folder and reference.fasta with your own paths. The adapter-containing
examples apply only when their listed terminal sequences are retained.

ONT cDNA / direct-cDNA:
    LoRTIA -5 TGCCATTAGGCCGGG --five_score 16 --check_in_soft 15 \
      --check_in_match 10 --check_from_alignment 3 -3 AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA \
      --three_score 16 --shs_for_ts 3 -s poisson \
      sample.sorted.bam output_folder reference.fasta

ONT CapTrap:
    LoRTIA -5 AACGCAGAGTAC --five_score 16 --check_in_soft 15 \
      --check_in_match 10 --check_from_alignment 3 -3 AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA \
      --three_score 16 --shs_for_ts 3 -s poisson \
      sample.sorted.bam output_folder reference.fasta

PacBio cDNA / Iso-Seq (5' adapter retained):
    LoRTIA -5 AGAGTACATGGG --five_score 16 --check_in_soft 15 \
      --check_in_match 10 --check_from_alignment 3 -3 AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA \
      --three_score 18 --shs_for_ts 3 -s poisson \
      sample.sorted.bam output_folder reference.fasta

PacBio cDNA (5' primer removed; terminal poly(A) retained):
    LoRTIA --pacbio --check_in_soft 15 --check_in_match 10 \
      --check_from_alignment 3 -3 AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA --three_score 18 \
      --shs_for_ts 3 -s poisson \
      sample.sorted.bam output_folder reference.fasta

PacBio CapTrap (5' adapter retained):
    LoRTIA -5 AACGCAGAGTAC --five_score 16 --check_in_soft 15 \
      --check_in_match 10 --check_from_alignment 3 -3 AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA \
      --three_score 16 --shs_for_ts 3 -s poisson \
      sample.sorted.bam output_folder reference.fasta

ONT direct RNA:
    LoRTIA --dRNA --check_in_soft 6 --check_in_match 10 \
      --check_from_alignment 3 -3 AAAA --three_score 6 --shs_for_ts 12 \
      -s poisson sample.sorted.bam output_folder reference.fasta

Upstream alignment note:
    Preserve terminal query sequence in supplementary alignments. For example,
    use minimap2 with -Y and --secondary=no. ONT direct-RNA alignments should
    additionally use the appropriate splice-aware direct-RNA options. For
    BAM-based workflows, use a valid alignment that preserves terminal query
    sequence; the benchmark workflows used coordinate-sorted and indexed BAMs.

These are recommended starting configurations, not universal optima. Detailed
chemistry-specific guidance and exact benchmark-specific commands are provided
in Supplementary Table S2 and Supplementary Table S4, respectively.

For detailed implementation-level definitions and parameter interactions, see
the LoRTIA Plus supplementary documentation and repository documentation.
"""


